A||||9||FieroLisa||Non Fiero tech question (mazda)||||1||
Z||000000||FieroLisa||08-12-2001||08:57 PM||||OK I know I should be posting this in O/T but a lot of people are not really checking it out there cause of the arguing there- and I need some sort of help here.  On a 1999 Mazda Protege, the check engine light came on. Is there a way for me to figure out what the codes are? <BR>Thanks, and sorry for technically posting this in the wrong section.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||08-12-2001||09:14 PM||||Easy solution for you... buy my 323GTX and put its B6T turbo 16v engine in the Protege! What a country!<P>Seriously, on Yahoo groups there are several Mazda related groups, including somethat specialize in Proteges.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WampusCat||08-12-2001||09:24 PM||||The most common reason on most newer cars, especially the Mazda's of friends I know is a un-tightened fuel cap.  Try tightening the gas cap until it clicks a couple of times and see if that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroLisa||08-12-2001||10:59 PM||||Wampus Cat-thanks, but already checked that fuel cap, and the fuse for the computer.  There is a ping, which is probably from bad gas so we just put some gas treatment in and refilled the tank. <P>Ohh well||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||08-12-2001||11:54 PM||||Hi Lisa, 02 sensor?<P>Dunno of a way to get the ECU to read off the codes like on the fiero (but I bet it exists)...<P>Hunting around for a Scan Tool on the cheap wouldn't be a terrible idea!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steve Normington||08-13-2001||11:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>Thanks, and sorry for technically posting this in the wrong section.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Okay, but do it again and we'll subject you to a long and rambling thread.<P><A HREF="http://www.obd-2.com" TARGET=_blank>This site</A> has an adapter that hooks into a 9-pin serial port to download trouble codes. The price is $122 for the hookup and includes software. I'm using it for my 97 3.4 L computer and it is working quite well. It doesn't require much in the way of computer (I'm using an old 486).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||08-13-2001||12:23 PM||||mazda and ford are interconnected pretty good. might be an avenue to consider for info.||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||08-14-2001||03:22 PM||||If you tightened the cap after the computer registered an error you need to reset the cpu.  Unplug the positive batt cable for 15 minutes and see if that clears the error.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Indyrestoguy_JR||08-14-2001||03:42 PM||||Lisa,<P>If it has a 'ping' it may have an anti-knock sensor hooked to the computer. Run the next higher plug (heat range) and reset the computer if the 'ping' goes away.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||08-14-2001||04:25 PM||||A lot of OBD-2 systems generally need the scan tool to read the codes.  I do think, however, that there are people on the Probe/MX6 message board who have read the their codes without one.  Since Probes use Mazda engines, you might want to do a search in the repair section of Probetalk.com. Maybe they work the same as the Protege. ||||reg||1
